---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/for/b2b-wholesale';
const title = "Shopify email apps for B2B wholesale: account states and rep handoffs";
const desc = "Choose wholesale email around company locations, approval, catalog permissions, rep ownership and reorder exceptions rather than generic DTC rankings.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which app is best for wholesale?",
    "answer": "The fit depends on location-aware access, sales ownership and the connector data you can verify. Demonstrate those requirements before selecting from a shortlist; a generic ecommerce ranking does not establish B2B suitability."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should approval start marketing automatically?",
    "answer": "Approval changes account access. Evaluate marketing eligibility separately and recheck both states before sending a campaign."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should a rep stop automated follow-up?",
    "answer": "Use a documented account or location state with an owner, blocked journeys and a clear resume action. Test an open quote and an unanswered reply before release."
  },
  {
    "question": "How do we avoid DTC collisions?",
    "answer": "Preserve company and location context, catalog eligibility and campaign purpose. Test a person who has both a consumer identity and wholesale access instead of relying only on a broad wholesale tag."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a class="link" href="/for">← Store types</a>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Store workflow guide</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Wholesale email starts with the account relationship. A person can buy for multiple locations, a company can have several buyers, and sales may pause while terms or a quote are being negotiated. Choose a platform only after proving that it can represent those states without sending consumer offers to trade accounts.</p>
      </header>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Model company, location and contact separately</h2>
        <p>Shopify’s <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/b2b/companies-and-customers/creating-companies">company documentation</a> describes location-specific settings including pricing, payment terms and contacts. Its <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/b2b/companies-and-customers/adding-customers">contact guide</a> covers assigning customers to locations and permissions. A flat wholesale tag is therefore not a sufficient access model. Keep company and location identifiers alongside the individual contact, and record which location a message concerns.</p>
        <h2>Plan availability is not the integration contract</h2>
        <p>Use Shopify’s <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/b2b/getting-started/plan-features">current B2B plan matrix</a> to check native availability; do not assume every B2B feature requires Plus. Then inspect what the email connector actually exposes. A platform receiving orders does not automatically receive company permissions, catalog assignment or approval changes. Require a field map with source, destination, update direction and behavior when a value disappears.</p>
        <h2>Shortlist by the missing job</h2>
        <p>For a rep-led business, require a demonstration of quote status, assigned rep and automation pause/resume. For a self-serve portal, require location-aware catalog links and first-order suppression. For a custom API setup, require an owner for event delivery, retries and monitoring. Sequenzy, ActiveCampaign, Klaviyo, Brevo and Drip can enter your shortlist, but their names do not prove these account-specific requirements. Avoid ranking them from invented activation results or assuming pay-per-email economics.</p>
        <h2>Application to approval is an access transition</h2>
        <p>Keep pending applications out of approved-catalog campaigns. An application acknowledgement can explain the review process without promising access or a discount. On approval, provide the right portal instructions, location and rep contact. If approval is revoked while a sequence is waiting, recheck eligibility before the next message. An approved account that has not ordered may need help with terms or catalog access; it is not automatically an email failure.</p>
        <h2>First order belongs to a company location</h2>
        <p>Proposed rehearsal: approve a company with two locations and two contacts, then place an order for only one location. Verify which onboarding messages stop and which remain useful. Add a contact who is permitted to order for both locations, and check that the links and prices match the context. When a contact leaves the company, revoke access in the source and verify the downstream communication rule. Do not use personal email engagement as a substitute for account state.</p>
        <h2>Rep handoff must pause the right automation</h2>
        <p>Define states such as quote requested, rep working, waiting for buyer and returned to self-serve. Record who changes them and which messages they block. A reply about a negotiated price should reach the assigned rep rather than launch another catalog promotion. Before resuming automation, have the rep resolve the exception and confirm the relevant location. Audit missing rep assignments explicitly; quietly sending a generic nurture path is not a reliable fallback.</p>
        <h2>Reorder, quantity rules and payment terms</h2>
        <p>Build reorder candidates from location-level orders and product seasonality, then let an open quote, recent purchase or unresolved service issue suppress the reminder. State quantity requirements from the approved catalog context rather than guessing from the last order. Payment due dates and stock replenishment are different events: a receivables notice belongs to the finance process, while a reorder message should not imply a changed credit limit. Review exceptional terms with the account owner.</p>
        <h2>Trade show and mixed DTC audiences</h2>
        <p>Preserve the event source and what follow-up was requested. Give sales a way to qualify an event lead before granting portal access. Separate account approval from marketing eligibility; neither a badge scan nor a company record establishes every promotional permission. For contacts who also shop as consumers, test the specific location and campaign context so a retail discount does not contradict negotiated trade pricing.</p>
        <h2>Cost, migration and accountable measurement</h2>
        <p>Compare current quotes using billable contacts, expected sends, CRM seats and any custom company-data work. Count the same human and multiple account associations carefully. Move approval, rep ownership and suppression data with the identity map, then pause equivalent automations before enabling replacements. Report first orders per eligible approved location, reorder behavior and unresolved handoffs using explicit cohorts. Keep lead acquisition costs separate from software cost; no universal activation target or threefold ROI rule decides the move.</p>
      </article>
      <nav class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related guides">
        <a class="link" href="/use-cases/segmentation">Segmentation</a>
        <a class="link" href="/for/high-aov">High-AOV stores</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
